  "rootCause": {
    "category": "A_both_200_last_writer_wins_single_primary",
    "summary": "The pre-fix PUT handler's reset-then-set sequence (clear whoever is currently primary, then set the target row) ran inside one transaction each, so at every instant the DB genuinely held at most one isPrimary=true row -- the partial unique index was never violated. When transaction B's own updateMany ran after transaction A had already committed, B legitimately (not illegitimately) saw A's new primary as 'the current primary' and cleared it before setting its own row. Both HTTP calls returned 200; A's response body's claim of success was silently falsified by B's later commit.",
    "isUniqueIndexBroken": false,
    "isOperationSequencingBypassingIndex": false,
    "isTestSynchronizationInvalid": false,
    "isGenuineApplicationContractViolation": true,
    "whyCreatePathPassedButUpdatePathFailed": "PUT has one extra pre-transaction round trip (loading `existing`) absent from POST, making back-to-back (non-overlapping) transaction execution measurably more likely on GitHub Actions' runner timing -- exactly the ordering that triggers the domino instead of a genuine unique-index collision.",
